`@layer` adds a whole new tiebreaker **above** specificity: layers are checked before specificity is even considered, and a declaration in a later-declared layer beats one in an earlier layer regardless of which selector is more specific.

```css
@layer reset, base, components, utilities;

@layer base {
  .btn { padding: 8px 16px; }
}
@layer components {
  #sidebar .btn { padding: 4px; } /* loses anyway — wrong layer order */
}
```

The payoff: you can declare a strict layer order once (`reset, base, components, utilities`) and every rule inside the `utilities` layer will beat every rule in `components`, no matter how many IDs the `components` selector stacks up — specificity fights only happen *within* a layer, never across one. This is the mechanism design systems use to guarantee a `.hidden { display: none !important }`-style utility always wins without needing `!important` at all — put it in the last layer.
